## Running Locally

### Requirements

- Node.js 18+
- npm
- No API key required for the demo path

### Install

```powershell
cd $HOME\sitroom
npm.cmd install
```

### Start the Live UI

```powershell
npx.cmd tsx src/ui-server.ts
```

Then open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8787
```

Click **Run incident**.

### CLI Live Run

```powershell
npx.cmd tsx src/cli.ts --speed 4 --log logs/last.jsonl
```

### CLI Replay

```powershell
npx.cmd tsx src/cli.ts --replay logs/last.jsonl --speed 8
```

---

## Disclosure — What Is Staged

### Speech Is Canned

Speech consists of fixed lines emitted on a timer.

It is **not an LLM**.

### Kill Is Deterministic

The kill does not come from a model decision.

The rule is:

```text
deploy + last_deploy_hash + unchanged → kill
else → keep talking
```

### Join Is Fixture-Driven

Security pages on `tick-auth` because the fixture says so.

The join implementation itself is real:

```text
attach + pageIn in the same turn
```

### Replay Is the Reliability Path

The preferred demo is the live UI.

If the laptop hitches, use:

```powershell
npx.cmd tsx src/cli.ts --replay logs/last.jsonl --speed 4
```

Replay reads a real engine log. It is not a hand-written tombstone script.

---

## What Is Not Faked

The following behaviors are real:

- Shared event bus
- Isolated per-agent memory
- Concurrent token timestamps
- Dead agent emits zero tokens after kill
- Late joiner starts on the join tick
- JSONL write
- Identical replay fingerprint
- Per-specialist Mozaik `ModelContext`

